{"content":[{"c":["ESTHER (P",{"c":["erson"],"sourceClass":"lc","t":"span"},")"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["One of two names borne by the Jewish queen of Persia. Hadassah (Hebrew “Myrtle”) apparently was her Jewish name (",{"href":"?bref=Esth.2.7","r":"Esth.2.7","rawR":"Esth.2.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Esth.2.7","value":"Esth.2.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"Est 2:7"},"), and Esther (Persian “Star”) her name as queen of Persia. Some scholars speculate about a connection with the Babylonian goddess Ishtar, since exiled Jews were occasionally given pagan names (see ",{"href":"?bref=Dan.1.7","r":"Dan.1.7","rawR":"Dan.1.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Dan.1.7","value":"Dan.1.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"Dn 1:7"},")."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["Esther was an orphan from the tribe of Benjamin who lived with the Jewish exiles in Persia. She was reared by her cousin Mordecai, a minor government official and covert leader of the Jewish community (see ",{"href":"?bref=Esth.3.5-6","r":"Esth.3.5-6","rawR":"Esth.3.5-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Esth.3.5-6","value":"Esth.3.5-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Est 3:5-6"},") in Susa, capital of the Persian kingdom. Esther became queen after King Ahasuerus (Xerxes) became displeased with Queen Vashti when she refused to obey his command to attend a banquet (",{"href":"?bref=Esth.1.11-12","r":"Esth.1.11-12","rawR":"Esth.1.11-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Esth.1.11-12","value":"Esth.1.11-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:11-12"},")."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"},{"c":["After Esther’s coronation, she discreetly won Xerxes’ confidence by informing him of an assassination plot (",{"href":"?bref=Esth.2.21-23","r":"Esth.2.21-23","rawR":"Esth.2.21-23","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Esth.2.21-23","value":"Esth.2.21-23"}],"t":"ref","v":"Est 2:21-23"},"). The favor she won in the king’s eyes enabled her to deliver her family and her people from a massacre by Haman, a high official to the king."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"},{"c":["The Feast of Purim was instituted to celebrate God’s deliverance of his people through Esther and Mordecai. This festival is still observed annually by Jews."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"},{"c":[{"c":["See also"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," ",{"href":"?item=EstherBookof_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","id":"EstherBookof_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","rawId":"EstherBookof_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","t":"link","v":"Esther, Book of"},"."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"EstherPerson","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"Est 2:7","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Esth.2.7","rawQueryValue":"Esth.2.7","sourceRefExact":"Esth.2.7"},{"anchorText":"Dn 1:7","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Dan.1.7","rawQueryValue":"Dan.1.7","sourceRefExact":"Dan.1.7"},{"anchorText":"Est 3:5-6","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Esth.3.5-6","rawQueryValue":"Esth.3.5-6","sourceRefExact":"Esth.3.5-6"},{"anchorText":"1:11-12","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Esth.1.11-12","rawQueryValue":"Esth.1.11-12","sourceRefExact":"Esth.1.11-12"},{"anchorText":"Est 2:21-23","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Esth.2.21-23","rawQueryValue":"Esth.2.21-23","sourceRefExact":"Esth.2.21-23"}],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"b3b2074ad2f06d21c951b5287a4a59b9412ae1065bdb4ba083008e3d036e45f8","sourceItemName":"EstherPerson","sourceItemOrder":251,"sourceMember":"Articles/E.xml","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[]},"sourceAttributes":{"name":"EstherPerson","product":"TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","typename":"Article"},"sourceTitleExact":"Esther (Person)","title":"Esther (Person)","version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}